 ## Overview
-This method parameterizes unbounded 360-degree scenes into a bounded coordinate space, enabling the use of bounded data structures like voxel grids and neural fields for large-scale scene reconstruction [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of infinite scene extent by mapping $\mathbb{R}^3$ to a finite domain (typically a sphere or cube of radius 2), allocating model capacity proportional to disparity (inverse distance) so that distant content receives appropriate resolution [S1]. It sets itself apart from standard NeRF by explicitly handling the unbounded nature of indoor/outdoor 360° captures, whereas siblings like [Position-Only NeRF Field](1253-position-only-nerf-field.md) assume bounded or forward-facing constraints.
+This method parameterizes unbounded, inward-facing scenes by mapping coordinates from $\mathbb{R}^3$ into a bounded domain (typically a unit sphere or cube), enabling neural radiance fields to allocate model capacity proportional to disparity [S1, S3]. It solves the problem of infinite ray sampling and sparse distant content by compressing the infinite space into a finite volume where points at infinity map to the boundary [S1, S3]. It differs from standard bounded scene representations by explicitly handling the asymptotic behavior of rays, and from forward-facing parameterizations by using spherical or cuboid contraction rather than depth-layer warping [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** 3D world coordinates $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ (and optionally viewing direction $\mathbf{d}$, though the contraction applies to position).
-*   **Output:** Contracted coordinates $\mathbf{x}'$ within a bounded domain (e.g., $[-2, 2]^3$ or unit sphere).
+**Inputs:** 3D world coordinates $\mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^3$.
+**Outputs:** Contracted coordinates $\mathbf{x}' \in [-2, 2]^3$ (or bounded cuboid).
+**Data Structures:** None required for the core contraction function; often used with a voxel grid or occupancy grid [S2, S4, unofficial].
 
-**Data Structures:**
